#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adef789f-225b-5894-a8b9-5e11934c377c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306052} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1fd109-16af-5879-bcfd-4d1366864d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306053} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd4ab4f-95a1-5b0c-80a0-5c09c687f7aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306054} {:answer/id #uuid "abf597b9-e766-597c-b9d7-6faf4a80df03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306055} {:answer/id #uuid "6d418e2c-fc04-5c80-b390-b1fd8c7464a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abf597b9-e766-597c-b9d7-6faf4a80df03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306055} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc031990-69d2-56b3-825b-b891ab692887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80fd1b01-b4c3-5a42-879f-a9a61102a17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-21035" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=12x-7+\\frac{3}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}=0\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no real solutions, so there are no points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91fa8227-8c90-5d51-88b8-bcf1c351817f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306059} {:answer/id #uuid "8184564e-2354-5008-ba6d-40d01b38685c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306060} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef63f8a-a9b8-59c6-9bfc-926c2673eecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306061} {:answer/id #uuid "cafcb22b-eb24-5d42-8dab-22675689aee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306062} {:answer/id #uuid "32a0b238-15af-54e3-92c9-26f10cb04cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91fa8227-8c90-5d51-88b8-bcf1c351817f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306059} :answer-field/id #uuid "e96c7d1f-71a1-5ec6-98e5-2fd5d6f4684b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51c9af9b-7fc6-5955-bfbb-b82d2cff1bc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-21041" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=3x-7+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}(1-\\frac{3}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=3.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=2$ and $x=4$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(2)^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(4) & =\\frac{2}{(4)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(4)^{4}}=\\frac{1}{128}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=3,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d95b1835-d1ea-5052-bef1-441518bc6883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306066} {:answer/id #uuid "1c08b0ac-5955-5700-8bf2-c879f2200913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306067} {:answer/id #uuid "a252231e-cfa1-5bea-8219-390f4b756b2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306068} {:answer/id #uuid "57599abf-0cb2-5d02-9350-ef70e4d42ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306069} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf3001a-c60d-550a-a637-820b1ba811a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57599abf-0cb2-5d02-9350-ef70e4d42ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306069} :answer-field/id #uuid "398896fc-e61f-58b1-9a4a-7cc22e7db73b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "013aa5fa-d294-56b4-a075-76d80070e739" :question/math-academy-id "q-2464" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x+4.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4x+4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "decc4370-a24d-5c18-9598-95f621a07e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298959} {:answer/id #uuid "636e595b-851f-5d2c-9658-825bedb3b34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298960} {:answer/id #uuid "55705a75-81a9-594a-8ad8-92b37266c1f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298961} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0b3677-de6c-5e44-a12e-eef28d35cebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298962} {:answer/id #uuid "99a44cc3-3c65-5a9f-abeb-d3b5530603e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "636e595b-851f-5d2c-9658-825bedb3b34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298960} :answer-field/id #uuid "236ebe4f-089a-5ef0-a6cc-de2d76cf4d01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05af1cea-c88c-5fcc-8838-0055bd7f29aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31213" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/assets/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd912d0-af60-5de5-917e-56811ac061fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306073} {:answer/id #uuid "d5da1f00-098a-58e6-96f8-9b08133db7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306074} {:answer/id #uuid "94e71d33-30bd-5a00-af9c-6d61470a6f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306075} {:answer/id #uuid "9637da0e-1374-55ad-a37b-fd31c50f38e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306076} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cb004d-0eff-5541-912f-6e08c4ca9fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fd912d0-af60-5de5-917e-56811ac061fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306073} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cc558ef-5644-5852-a40b-9f436fa85c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "195b2308-1c8c-58f1-bee1-408b56e897dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79198" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/assets/d2a8410c92775ef68678a1cac187b3ebc717cfa9fa737ce9723c531616706717.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis only at at $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has point of inflection at $x=1.$"}]}
